---
import StandardPageLayout from "../../layouts/StandardPageLayout.astro";
import FinancialStatementKey from "../../components/FinancialStatementKey.astro";
import Glossary from "../../components/Glossary.astro";
import PageToc from "../../components/PageToc.astro";
import "../../styles/standards-poster.css";
import "../../styles/extended.css";
import HistoryTimeline from "../../components/HistoryTimeline.astro";
import history from "../../data/history.json";
import metadata from "../../data/standards/usgaap/asc905.json";
---

<StandardPageLayout
  code={metadata.code}
  standardType={metadata.framework}
  title={metadata.title}
  description={metadata.description}
  issued={metadata.issued}
  latestAmendment={metadata.latestAmendment}
  status={metadata.status}
  summary={metadata.summary}
  bannerNotice={metadata.bannerNotice}
>
  <Fragment slot="toc">
    <PageToc
      items={[
        { id: "s1", label: "1. Conceptual Framework Linkage" },
        { id: "s2", label: "2. Scope — Agricultural Producers and Cooperatives" },
        { id: "s3", label: "3. General Measurement — Historical Cost Remains the Default" },
        { id: "s4", label: "4. The Market Value (Net Realizable Value) Exception" },
        { id: "s5", label: "5. THE KEY DIVERGENCE — No Comprehensive Biological Asset Fair Value Model" },
        { id: "s6", label: "6. Cooperative-Specific Accounting" },
        { id: "s7", label: "7. Worked Example — the Market Value Method for a Harvested Crop" },
        { id: "s8", label: "8. Worked Example — Contrasting IAS 41's Growing-Crop Fair Value Model" },
        { id: "s9", label: "9. Disclosure Requirements" },
        { id: "s10", label: "10. Critical Judgments &amp; Estimates" },
        { id: "s11", label: "11. ASC 905 vs IAS 41 — Comprehensive Comparison" },
        { id: "s12", label: "12. Statement Mapping" },
        { id: "s13", label: "13. Related Topics" },
        { id: "s14", label: "14. Glossary" }
      ]}
    />
  </Fragment>
  <div class="wrap">

    <div class="section-header" id="s1"><span class="num">1</span><h2>Conceptual Framework Linkage</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card ci"><div class="ctitle ci">A narrow, historically-rooted exception rather than a comprehensive framework</div><div class="principle">Agricultural products have LONG been recognised as a special case in accounting — once HARVESTED, crops and animals often have a READY market, a REASONABLY ASSURED price, negligible marketing costs, and are largely INTERCHANGEABLE, making the traditional historical-cost model less meaningful. ASC 905 addresses this through a NARROW, specific exception (Section 4) — but does NOT attempt a comprehensive remeasurement framework for agricultural assets more broadly.</div></div>
    <div class="card cd"><div class="ctitle cd">A major structural gap compared to IAS 41</div><div class="principle">IAS 41 (Agriculture) takes a FUNDAMENTALLY different, far more comprehensive approach — requiring virtually ALL biological assets to be remeasured to FAIR VALUE at EVERY reporting date, throughout their entire growth cycle, with changes flowing through PROFIT OR LOSS. US GAAP has NO equivalent standard-wide biological asset fair value model — this is one of the most SUBSTANTIAL remaining measurement-basis divergences anywhere in the Codification (Section 5).</div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s2"><span class="num">2</span><h2>Scope — Agricultural Producers and Cooperatives</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">Crops, livestock, and agricultural cooperative entities</div><div class="principle">ASC 905 applies to entities engaged in AGRICULTURAL PRODUCER activities (growing crops, raising livestock) and to AGRICULTURAL COOPERATIVES (entities owned by and operated for the benefit of their member-producers, e.g. grain elevators, dairy cooperatives).</div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s3"><span class="num">3</span><h2>General Measurement — Historical Cost Remains the Default</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Growing crops and immature animals generally follow ordinary cost accumulation</div><div class="principle">As a GENERAL rule, growing crops and IMMATURE (not-yet-harvested/not-yet-mature) animals continue to be accounted for at ACCUMULATED HISTORICAL COST — the costs of seed, feed, labor, and other direct/allocable costs are accumulated as the crop grows or the animal matures, much like WORK-IN-PROCESS inventory in a manufacturing context. There is <strong>NO general requirement to remeasure growing crops or immature livestock to fair value</strong> during their growth phase under US GAAP.</div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s4"><span class="num">4</span><h2>The Market Value (Net Realizable Value) Exception</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">A narrow exception at, or near, the point of harvest/completion</div><div class="principle">Certain agricultural products — MOST commonly at or near HARVEST or completion of growth — may be stated at SALES (MARKET) PRICE, LESS DISPOSAL COSTS, even ABOVE cost (recognising a gain BEFORE actual sale), PROVIDED specific criteria are met: (a) the product has a RELIABLE, READILY DETERMINABLE market price; (b) the product has RELATIVELY INSIGNIFICANT and PREDICTABLE disposal costs; and (c) the product is available for IMMEDIATE delivery. This "completed-production" or "market price" method is a narrow, LONG-STANDING exception to the normal lower-of-cost-(or-market) inventory principle, applicable mainly to certain grains, some precious metals, and select commodities that meet these specific criteria.</div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s5"><span class="num">5</span><h2>THE KEY DIVERGENCE — No Comprehensive Biological Asset Fair Value Model</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">IAS 41's mandatory, comprehensive fair value remeasurement — with no US GAAP equivalent</div><div class="principle">IAS 41 requires an entity to measure BIOLOGICAL ASSETS (living plants and animals) — and, in most cases, AGRICULTURAL PRODUCE at the point of harvest — at <strong>FAIR VALUE LESS COSTS TO SELL</strong>, at EVERY reporting date, THROUGHOUT the ENTIRE growth cycle (not merely at or near harvest), with ALL changes in fair value (both from PRICE changes and from PHYSICAL growth/transformation) recognised DIRECTLY in PROFIT OR LOSS as they occur — a genuinely COMPREHENSIVE, MANDATORY fair value model (subject to a narrow cost-based exception only where fair value cannot be reliably measured). <strong>US GAAP has NO PARALLEL, standard-wide biological asset fair value framework</strong> — ASC 905's narrow market-value exception (Section 4) applies only NARROWLY, generally near completion/harvest, under SPECIFIC qualifying criteria, and growing crops/immature livestock are otherwise carried at HISTORICAL COST throughout their growth phase. This represents one of the MOST SUBSTANTIAL remaining measurement-basis divergences between the two frameworks anywhere in this library — a company's DAIRY HERD, ORCHARD, or STANDING TIMBER could be measured on an ENTIRELY different basis (continuously remeasured fair value vs accumulated historical cost) purely depending on which framework applies.</div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s6"><span class="num">6</span><h2>Cooperative-Specific Accounting</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cs span2"><div class="ctitle cs">Patronage dividends and marketing arrangements</div><div class="principle">Agricultural COOPERATIVES have their own specific accounting considerations — notably PATRONAGE DIVIDENDS (distributions to member-producers based on the volume of business each member transacted with the cooperative, rather than on ownership shares as such), and specific guidance on accounting for MARKETING arrangements between the cooperative and its member-producers.</div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s7"><span class="num">7</span><h2>Worked Example — the Market Value Method for a Harvested Crop</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">Recognising a gain before sale, under narrow criteria</div>
    <div class="worked" style="margin-bottom:0;"><div class="wk-title">Example: Harvested wheat with a reliable market price</div><div class="wk-body">A grain producer harvests wheat with an accumulated cost of $400,000. The wheat has a well-established commodity market price, insignificant and predictable transportation/handling costs of $15,000, and is available for immediate delivery to buyers. Its market value less disposal costs is $460,000.</div><div class="wk-answer">Since the wheat meets ALL the specific qualifying criteria (reliable market price, insignificant/predictable disposal costs, available for immediate delivery), the producer may state the wheat at $460,000 — recognising a <strong>$60,000 gain</strong> ($460,000 − $400,000) BEFORE actual sale, under the narrow market value exception.</div></div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s8"><span class="num">8</span><h2>Worked Example — Contrasting IAS 41's Growing-Crop Fair Value Model</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">The same orchard, two fundamentally different measurement bases</div>
    <div class="worked" style="margin-bottom:0;"><div class="wk-title">Example: A maturing apple orchard, not yet harvested</div><div class="wk-body">An entity owns an apple orchard, with trees still several months from harvest. Accumulated historical cost (fertilizer, labor, water) to date is $2,000,000. An appraiser estimates the trees' fair value less costs to sell, reflecting current market conditions and the trees' growth stage, at $2,650,000.</div><div class="wk-answer"><strong>Under US GAAP (ASC 905):</strong> the orchard remains at its <strong>$2,000,000 ACCUMULATED HISTORICAL COST</strong> — the trees are not yet harvested, so the narrow market value exception does NOT apply; NO remeasurement gain is recognised.<br><strong>Under IAS 41:</strong> the orchard (a biological asset) is remeasured to its <strong>$2,650,000 FAIR VALUE LESS COSTS TO SELL</strong>, recognising a <strong>$650,000 gain</strong> DIRECTLY in profit or loss — EVEN THOUGH the trees are still growing and no harvest or sale has occurred.</div></div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s9"><span class="num">9</span><h2>Disclosure Requirements</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">What ASC 905 requires</div><div class="check-list"><span>Accounting policies used for inventories of agricultural products, including whether the market value method is applied</span><span>Nature of patronage dividend arrangements for cooperatives</span></div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s10"><span class="num">10</span><h2>Critical Judgments &amp; Estimates</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cn span2"><div class="ctitle cn">Where judgement matters most</div>
    <div class="two-col"><div><div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT JUDGEMENTS</div><div class="dot-list"><span>Whether a harvested product meets all three qualifying criteria for the market value method</span></div></div>
    <div><div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT ESTIMATES</div><div class="dot-list"><span>Estimated disposal costs used in determining net realizable value under the market value method</span></div></div></div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s11"><span class="num">11</span><h2>ASC 905 vs IAS 41 — Comprehensive Comparison</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">Comparison — ASC 905 vs IAS 41</div>
    <div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>Feature</span><span style="color:var(--color-text-warning);">US GAAP (ASC 905)</span><span style="color:var(--color-text-info);">IFRS (IAS 41)</span></div>
    <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>Growing crops / immature livestock</span><span>Historical cost accumulation</span><span>Fair value less costs to sell, remeasured EVERY period</span></div>
    <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>Harvested/mature agricultural products</span><span>NARROW market value exception, only if specific criteria met</span><span>Fair value less costs to sell, generally at point of harvest as the new cost basis for subsequent inventory accounting</span></div>
    <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>Where fair value changes are recognised</span><span>N/A generally, until the narrow exception applies</span><span>Directly in profit or loss, every period, throughout the growth cycle</span></div>
    <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>Scope breadth</span><span>Narrow — no comprehensive biological asset standard</span><span>Comprehensive — dedicated standard covering essentially all biological assets</span></div>
    </div>
    <div class="note">This is one of the most CONSEQUENTIAL measurement-basis divergences in the entire library — not a difference in mechanics applied to a shared principle, but the near-total ABSENCE of a comparable framework on the US GAAP side.</div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s12"><span class="num">12</span><h2>Statement Mapping</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cs span2"><div class="ctitle cs">Where every ASC 905 event lands</div>
    <div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Event</span><span>Statement(s) affected</span><span>Notes</span></div>
    <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Accumulated cost of growing crops/immature animals</span><span><span class="badge bs">BS</span></span><span>Carried as inventory at accumulated cost</span></div>
    <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Market value method gain (qualifying harvested products)</span><span><span class="badge bs">BS</span> <span class="badge bp">IS</span></span><span>Recognised before sale, under narrow criteria</span></div>
    </div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s13"><span class="num">13</span><h2>Related Topics</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Cross-reference map</div>
    <div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>Topic</span><span>Connection</span></div>
    <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>ASC 330</span><span>Inventory — supplies the general historical cost and lower-of-cost-or-market framework that ASC 905's exception departs from</span></div>
    <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>ASC 820</span><span>Supplies the fair value measurement framework used where fair value is determined under the narrow exception</span></div>
    </div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s14"><span class="num">14</span><h2>Quick-Reference Glossary</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cn span2">
    <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Market value (completed-production) method</div><div class="glossary-def">A narrow exception allowing certain harvested agricultural products meeting specific criteria to be stated at market price less disposal costs, even above cost.</div></div>
    <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Biological asset</div><div class="glossary-def">A living plant or animal — the subject of IAS 41's comprehensive fair value remeasurement model, with no equivalent standard-wide framework under US GAAP.</div></div>
    <div class="glossary-item" style="border-bottom:none;"><div class="glossary-term">Patronage dividend</div><div class="glossary-def">A distribution by an agricultural cooperative to its member-producers based on the volume of business each member transacted with the cooperative.</div></div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <FinancialStatementKey />

  </div>
  <section id="history">
    <HistoryTimeline items={history.asc905 || []} />
  </section>
</StandardPageLayout>
